/* Majlis — assessment question banks (data only). Loaded before app.js. */
const PHIL_QUESTIONS = [
  { q: 'The trolley problem (Philippa Foot): a runaway trolley will kill five people unless you divert it onto a side track, where it will kill one. Should you divert it?', opts: ['Yes. Consequentialism: the right act is the one with the best outcome, and five lives outweigh one', 'Yes, but it leaves moral residue: diverting is right, yet killing the one is still a wrong to regret (Bernard Williams)', 'No. The doctrine of doing and allowing: actively killing one is worse than letting five die', 'There is no fact of the matter. Emotivism: calling an act right only expresses our feelings about it (A.J. Ayer)'] },
  { q: 'Kant\'s categorical imperative: act only on a rule you could will everyone to follow. Is that the right test of a moral action?', opts: ['Yes. Morality is a matter of duties that bind us whatever the consequences (Kant)', 'Largely. There are real duties, but they can conflict, and judgement decides which wins (W.D. Ross)', 'Only as a rule of thumb. Rules matter because following them generally has good results (rule consequentialism)', 'No. Only consequences matter, and any rule should be broken when breaking it does more good (act utilitarianism)'] },
  { q: 'Plato\'s Ring of Gyges: a ring makes you invisible, so you could do wrong and never be caught. Would you still have a reason to be just?', opts: ['Yes. Justice is good in itself, whatever it brings, and the unjust soul is harmed by its injustice (Plato)', 'Yes. Being just is part of being a good, flourishing person, so injustice would damage my life (virtue ethics)', 'Only for what it brings. Justice is an agreement we keep because everyone gains from cooperation (Hobbes)', 'No. Justice is a convention that protects the weak, and with the ring it would be rational to ignore it (Glaucon, Thrasymachus)'] },
  { q: 'W.K. Clifford said it is wrong to believe anything on insufficient evidence. William James replied that some beliefs may rightly go beyond the evidence. Who is right?', opts: ['Clifford. It is wrong always, everywhere and for anyone to believe on insufficient evidence', 'Mostly Clifford, though we may act on probable beliefs when a decision can\'t wait', 'James. Where evidence can\'t settle a question and the choice matters, we may believe by an act of will', 'Neither goes far enough. Some of the deepest truths are known by the heart, not by evidence (Pascal)'] },
  { q: 'Derek Parfit\'s teleporter: a machine scans you, destroys your body, and builds an exact copy on Mars with all your memories. Is the person on Mars you?', opts: ['Yes. Personal identity is psychological continuity: same memories and personality, same person (Locke)', 'Identity isn\'t what matters. What matters is that my psychology carries on, and it does (Parfit)', 'No. I would die, and a new person would be made. A self is more than the information it contains', 'The question has no determinate answer. Our idea of \'the same person\' breaks down in cases like this'] },
  { q: 'Descartes\' evil demon: you cannot rule out that a powerful demon is deceiving you about everything you perceive. Can we know anything about the outside world?', opts: ['Yes, certainly. I know I have hands better than I could ever know a sceptical premise (G.E. Moore)', 'Yes. Knowledge needs reliable, well-justified belief, not certainty (fallibilism)', 'Only in a weak sense. The sceptic can\'t be refuted, so our knowledge rests on assumptions we can\'t prove', 'No. Radical scepticism is right, and we should suspend judgement about the outside world (Pyrrho, Sextus Empiricus)'] },
  { q: 'The central question of metaethics: are there objective moral truths, true whatever anyone believes?', tag: 'morality', opts: ['Yes. Moral realism: some moral claims, such as \'torturing for fun is wrong\', are objectively true', 'Yes, grounded in human nature: moral truths are facts about what makes humans flourish (Aristotle, Philippa Foot)', 'Only relative ones. Moral truths exist, but only relative to a culture or an agreement (relativism, constructivism)', 'No. Error theory: there are no moral facts, so every moral claim is false (J.L. Mackie)'] },
  { q: 'The problem of free will: if every event, including every choice, is caused by earlier events, can people be morally responsible for what they do?', tag: 'freewill', opts: ['Yes, because our choices are not fully caused. Libertarian free will: we could have chosen otherwise (Kant, Thomas Reid)', 'Yes. Compatibilism: you are free when you act on your own reasons, even if those reasons were caused (Hume, Frankfurt)', 'Only partly. Determinism means no one truly deserves blame, though praise and blame are still useful', 'No. Hard determinism: no one could have done otherwise, so no one truly deserves blame (Spinoza, Pereboom)'] },
  { q: 'Are intuitions evidence? When something just seems true to us, as many moral and philosophical claims do, does that count as a reason to believe it?', opts: ['No. Intuitions are products of bias and upbringing; only empirical evidence counts', 'A little. They are weak evidence, to be checked against theory and data', 'We can\'t tell. Intuitions differ across people and cultures, so we should suspend judgement', 'Yes. Some truths in ethics and mathematics are known directly by intuition (intuitionism, G.E. Moore)'] },
  { q: 'Cultural relativism: anthropologists find very different moral codes in different cultures. Does this show there is no universal morality?', opts: ['No. Disagreement doesn\'t mean there\'s no truth, and reason can settle who is right (Kant)', 'No. Beneath the differences lie shared human values; cultures mostly differ in circumstances (James Rachels)', 'Partly. A few core values may be universal, but much of morality is genuinely relative', 'Yes. Right and wrong are whatever a culture\'s code says (\'custom is king of all\', Herodotus)'] },
  { q: 'Can the mind survive the death of the body? (Plato\'s Phaedo against the materialists)', opts: ['Yes. The soul is a non-physical substance that can exist without the body (Plato, Descartes)', 'Possibly. If the mind is more than the brain, something of the person may continue (Aquinas)', 'No. The mind is what the brain does, so it ends when the brain does (physicalism, Epicurus)', 'We cannot know. Questions like this lie beyond the limits of possible knowledge (Kant)'] },
  { q: 'Philosophy of religion: does belief in God need argument or evidence to be rational?', opts: ['No. Faith is its own way of knowing and needs no outside support (fideism, Kierkegaard)', 'No. Belief in God can be \'properly basic\', rational without argument, like belief in other minds (Alvin Plantinga)', 'Yes, and there are good arguments for it (natural theology, Aquinas\' Five Ways)', 'Yes, and without strong evidence we should not believe it (evidentialism, Bertrand Russell)'] },
  { q: 'The problem of taste (Hume, Kant): is beauty a real feature of things, or only in the eye of the beholder?', opts: ['Real. Beauty is a property of things, which trained judgement can detect (Plato, Aquinas)', 'In between. Judgements of beauty rest on feeling, yet rightly claim everyone\'s agreement (Kant, Hume\'s standard of taste)', 'Mostly cultural. Standards of beauty come from the traditions we are raised in', 'In the eye of the beholder. Beauty is purely personal taste'] },
  { q: 'Philosophy of history: does human history show real progress?', opts: ['Yes. Reason and science drive steady moral and material progress (Condorcet, Hegel)', 'Mostly, though progress is fragile and can be reversed (Kant\'s cautious hope)', 'Only in our tools. Technology advances, but human nature does not (Schopenhauer)', 'No. History has no direction, and every gain brings a new loss (Rousseau, cyclical views of history)'] },
  { q: 'Philosophy of punishment: what justifies punishing someone who has broken the law?', opts: ['Retribution: wrongdoers deserve to suffer in proportion to their crime (Kant)', 'Mostly what the wrongdoer deserves, though mercy and remorse can rightly reduce it (mixed theories, H.L.A. Hart)', 'Deterrence and protection: punishment is justified only by the harm it prevents (Bentham)', 'Nothing but reform: blame is pointless, so punishment should only rehabilitate'] },
];

